\title{Infinite Proof Trees, Cyclic Dependencies, and Ordinal Descent\\
\large A Separation Theorem for Approximation, Circular Syntax, and Derivability}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{16 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Self-reference in proofs is often compared with recursion: a finite cyclic description may unfold into an infinite object, suggesting that circular derivations could acquire validity as limits of finite approximations. We isolate the mathematical ingredients of that suggestion and obtain a guarded conclusion. We define a minimal implicational calculus, address-labelled potentially infinite trees, finite-depth truncations, an information order on tree observations, and ordinal-ranked dependency graphs. The usual derivation of $P\to P$ has height one and is hypothetical rather than circular. A one-node cyclic graph unravels into a legitimate infinite unary tree with nodes at every finite depth. Observations ordered by inclusion form a complete lattice, and the union of a countable chain is its least upper bound. In contrast, any dependency relation that strictly decreases an ordinal rank on every edge has neither self-loops nor finite directed cycles. Strict ordinal descent therefore excludes genuine circularity rather than validating it. Finally, exact reflection $Q\leftrightarrow L$ is inconsistent with the liar equation $L\leftrightarrow\neg Q$. The results distinguish infinite proof-shaped objects from sound derivability and motivate guarded semantics and global trace conditions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A finite graph may describe infinite behavior. A loop in an automaton generates an unbounded run; a recursive equation may define an infinite stream; and a finite cyclic term may unravel into an infinite tree. It is natural to ask whether a proof may similarly refer to itself and acquire validity through a fixed-point construction.

Three questions must be separated. First, can a finite cyclic object denote a well-defined infinite tree? Second, do finite observations of that tree converge in a controlled order-theoretic sense? Third, does the resulting tree constitute a valid proof under specified inference rules? The first two concern representation and approximation. The third concerns soundness, and does not follow automatically from the first two.

A further ambiguity concerns hypotheses. In proving $A\to B$, one temporarily assumes $A$ and derives $B$. In the special case $P\to P$, the assumption and immediate conclusion are both $P$. Informally this may sound like ``using $P$ to prove $P$,'' but the derivation does not point back to its root. It is finite and well founded.

Finally, one might try to discipline a circular dependency by assigning ordinal ranks and demanding that every premise or back-reference have smaller rank than the node that uses it. We show that this condition cannot hold around a cycle. Edgewise strict descent is an acyclicity principle, not a circular fixed-point principle.

The analysis below develops a common framework for all three distinctions. After defining formulas, trees, observations, and ranked graphs, we establish the height-one identity derivation, the existence and unboundedness of a unary self-unravelling, least-upper-bound laws for observations, the impossibility of ordinal-ranked cycles, and the incompatibility of exact reflection with the liar equation.

\section{Mathematical framework}

\subsection{Formulas, sequents, and derivations}

Fix a set $\mathcal A$ of atomic proposition symbols.

\begin{definition}[Implicational formulas]
The set $\mathcal F$ is generated by
\[
 A ::= a \mid (A\to A),\qquad a\in\mathcal A.
\]
Thus atoms are formulas, and $A\to B$ is a formula whenever $A$ and $B$ are formulas.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Sequents]
A context $\Gamma$ is a finite list of formulas. A sequent is an expression $\Gamma\vdash A$, meaning that $A$ is to be derived from the open assumptions in $\Gamma$.
\end{definition}

We use the hypothesis and implication-introduction rules
\[
\frac{A\in\Gamma}{\Gamma\vdash A}\;\mathrm{Hyp}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\frac{A,\Gamma\vdash B}{\Gamma\vdash A\to B}\;{\to}I.
\]

\begin{definition}[Derivation height]
A derivation is a finite tree generated by the displayed rules. A hypothesis leaf has height $0$. An implication-introduction node has height one greater than its premise.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Address-labelled trees}

Let $\Lambda$ be a set of labels. An address is a finite list of natural numbers,
\[
p=(i_1,\ldots,i_k)\in\mathbb N^{<\omega},
\]
with depth $|p|=k$. The empty address denotes the root, and appending $i$ selects the $i$th child.

\begin{definition}[Possibly infinite labelled tree]
A labelled tree is an extensional function
\[
T:\mathbb N^{<\omega}\longrightarrow \Lambda\cup\{\bot\},
\]
where $T(p)=\bot$ means that no labelled node occurs at $p$.
\end{definition}

This broad definition does not impose prefix closure or inference-rule correctness. Those may be imposed later as predicates.

\begin{definition}[Truncation]
For $n\in\mathbb N$, the depth-$n$ truncation of $T$ is
\[
T_{\le n}(p)=
\begin{cases}
T(p),&|p|\le n,\\
\bot,&|p|>n.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\subsection{Observations and information order}

\begin{definition}[Observation]
An observation is a set
\[
X\subseteq\mathbb N^{<\omega}\times\Lambda.
\]
The pair $(p,a)$ records label $a$ at address $p$. Observations are ordered by information inclusion: $X\sqsubseteq Y$ exactly when $X\subseteq Y$.
\end{definition}

The ambient space includes partial and inconsistent observations. For a sequence $(C_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$, put
\[
\operatorname{Lim}(C)=\bigcup_{n\in\mathbb N}C_n.
\]
The sequence is increasing when $C_n\subseteq C_{n+1}$ for every $n$.

\subsection{Ranked dependency graphs}

\begin{definition}[Ordinal-ranked graph]
Let $V$ be a set and $D\subseteq V\times V$. We read $xDy$ as ``$x$ uses $y$ as a dependency.'' An ordinal ranking is a map $\rho:V\to\mathrm{Ord}$ such that
\[
xDy\Longrightarrow\rho(y)<\rho(x).
\]
\end{definition}

A finite directed cycle is a sequence $v_0,\ldots,v_n$ with
\[
v_0Dv_1,\ v_1Dv_2,\ldots,\ v_{n-1}Dv_n,\ v_nDv_0.
\]
A self-loop is $xDx$.

\section{Hypothetical reasoning is not circularity}

\begin{theorem}[Height-one identity derivation]
For every formula $P$, the sequent
\[
\varnothing\vdash P\to P
\]
has a derivation of height exactly $1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Add $P$ to the empty context. The hypothesis rule gives $P\vdash P$ at height $0$. Applying implication introduction discharges the assumption and yields $\varnothing\vdash P\to P$, increasing the height to $1$. An implication conclusion in this calculus uses implication introduction, so the displayed derivation has precisely the claimed height.
\end{proof}

The premise of implication introduction is a smaller derivation tree. Its leaf records membership in the context; it is not an edge to the root derivation. Thus the temporary assumption $P$ should not be confused with a circular citation of the theorem $P\to P$.

\section{Infinite unravellings and truncations}

\begin{theorem}[Eventual visibility]
For every labelled tree $T$ and address $p$,
\[
T_{\le |p|}(p)=T(p).
\]
More generally, the equality holds for every cutoff $n\ge |p|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The truncation changes a value only when the address depth exceeds the cutoff. Since $|p|\le |p|$, the original value is retained at cutoff $|p|$, and similarly at every larger cutoff.
\end{proof}

Consequently, $T_{\le0},T_{\le1},\ldots$ converges pointwise by stabilization: each fixed address eventually has its final value.

Fix $a\in\Lambda$.

\begin{definition}[Unary self-unravelling]
Define $U_a$ by
\[
U_a(p)=
\begin{cases}
a,&\text{if every entry of $p$ is $0$},\\
\bot,&\text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]
This is the unravelling of a one-node graph whose sole outgoing edge returns to that node.
\end{definition}

Let $0^n$ denote the address made from $n$ copies of $0$.

\begin{lemma}[Unary spine]
For every $n\in\mathbb N$,
\[
U_a(0^n)=a.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Every entry of $0^n$ is $0$, so the first clause of the definition applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded depth]
For every $n\in\mathbb N$, the tree $U_a$ has a labelled node at depth exactly $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose $p=0^n$. Then $|p|=n$ and the unary-spine lemma gives $U_a(p)=a$.
\end{proof}

Thus finite cyclic syntax may denote a genuine infinite object. The construction is extensional: it specifies the value at every finite address and never requires a set-theoretic node literally to contain itself. But this theorem concerns shape, not proof validity. No local inference rules have yet been imposed on the labels.

\section{Limits of finite information}

\begin{theorem}[Least upper bound of observations]
Let $(C_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ be any sequence of observations and define
\[
C_\infty=\bigcup_{n\in\mathbb N}C_n.
\]
Then $C_n\subseteq C_\infty$ for every $n$. Moreover, if $C_n\subseteq U$ for every $n$, then $C_\infty\subseteq U$. Hence $C_\infty$ is the least upper bound of the sequence.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every element of $C_n$ belongs to the union, proving the upper-bound property. Conversely, if $x\in C_\infty$, then $x\in C_n$ for some $n$. Every common upper bound $U$ contains that stage, hence contains $x$.
\end{proof}

No monotonicity assumption is required for the union to be the supremum of the family. For increasing sequences, information additionally persists between stages.

\begin{lemma}[Persistence]
If $C_n\subseteq C_{n+1}$ for every $n$, then
\[
m\le n\Longrightarrow C_m\subseteq C_n.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induct over the number of successor steps from $m$ to $n$. The base case is reflexivity. At each successor, compose the inclusion already obtained with $C_k\subseteq C_{k+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Complete observation lattice]
Observations ordered by inclusion form a complete lattice. Arbitrary suprema are unions and arbitrary infima are intersections. In particular, every increasing countable chain has a least upper bound.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
A union contains every member of its family and is contained in every common upper bound by the elementwise argument above. Dually, the intersection is the greatest lower bound.
\end{proof}

The ambient lattice contains inconsistent observations, such as two distinct labels at one address. If $\mathsf{Correct}(X)$ identifies proof-compatible observations, a separate theorem is needed to establish
\[
\bigl(\forall n,\mathsf{Correct}(C_n)\bigr)
\Longrightarrow
\mathsf{Correct}\left(\bigcup_n C_n\right).
\]
The lattice theorem solves information assembly, not soundness.

\section{Strict ordinal descent excludes cycles}

\begin{theorem}[No strictly ranked self-reference]
If $xDy$ implies $\rho(y)<\rho(x)$, then no node depends on itself.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A self-loop $xDx$ would imply $\rho(x)<\rho(x)$, contradicting irreflexivity of strict ordinal order.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No strictly ranked finite cycle]
A dependency graph admitting an ordinal ranking that strictly decreases on every edge has no finite directed cycle.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Assume a cycle
\[
v_0Dv_1D\cdots Dv_nDv_0.
\]
The rank condition yields
\[
\rho(v_1)<\rho(v_0),\quad \rho(v_2)<\rho(v_1),\quad\ldots,
\quad \rho(v_0)<\rho(v_n).
\]
Transitivity around the cycle gives $\rho(v_0)<\rho(v_0)$, contradicting irreflexivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[A self-loop has no decreasing ranking]
If $xDx$, no ordinal-valued function can strictly decrease along every dependency edge.
\end{corollary}

The conclusion does not depend on access to larger transfinite ordinals. Returning to the identical graph node returns to its identical rank. Strict decrease therefore cannot both occur and close the loop.

The analogy with terminating recursion must be handled carefully. A recursive function can call itself on a smaller argument because each concrete call has a different state. Its state-dependency graph is well founded. For a literal graph back-edge to the same proof node, no analogous state change appears. Guarded semantics can restore one by interpreting the same node at successively smaller step indices.

\section{The liar under exact reflection}

Let $L$ be a proposition intended to say that it is not provable, and let $Q$ mean that $L$ is provable. Consider
\[
Q\leftrightarrow L
\tag{R}
\]
and
\[
L\leftrightarrow\neg Q.
\tag{N}
\]
The first equation demands exact reflection between truth and provability for this sentence; the second is the liar's negating fixed-point equation.

\begin{theorem}[Liar fixed-point impossibility]
No ordinary propositions $Q$ and $L$ satisfy both \emph{(R)} and \emph{(N)}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From $Q\to L$ in (R) and $L\to\neg Q$ in (N), obtain $Q\to\neg Q$, hence $\neg Q$. Conversely, (N) gives $\neg Q\to L$, and (R) gives $L\to Q$. Therefore $\neg Q\to Q$. Applying this implication to the established $\neg Q$ yields $Q$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No exact liar model]
There is no pair $(Q,L)$ in ordinary two-valued propositional semantics satisfying exact reflection and the negating fixed-point equation simultaneously.
\end{corollary}

This obstruction is sharper than an undefined ordinal height: the two equations have no model before ranking is considered. Avoidance requires weakening reflection, stratifying provability, restricting fixed-point formation, or changing the semantics.

\section{Algorithms and finite experiments}

The structural results admit transparent finite illustrations.

\paragraph{Unary truncation.}
Given a cutoff $d$, enumerate $0^0,0^1,\ldots,0^d$ and attach label $a$. There are $d+1$ nodes. Explicit tuple construction uses $O(d^2)$ symbol writes and output space, whereas a stream of depth--label pairs uses $O(d)$ output and $O(1)$ auxiliary space.

\paragraph{Observation union.}
Given finite stages $C_0,\ldots,C_N$, initialize $U=\varnothing$ and update $U\leftarrow U\cup C_n$. With hash sets and $M=\sum_n|C_n|$, expected time is $O(M)$ and space is $O(|U|)$. The procedure realizes the least-upper-bound proof element by element.

\paragraph{Cycle detection and ranking.}
For a finite graph, depth-first search detects cycles in $O(|V|+|E|)$ time and $O(|V|)$ space. If the graph is acyclic, reverse topological evaluation defines a natural-number rank
\[
\rho(x)=
\begin{cases}
0,&x\text{ has no dependencies},\\
1+\max\{\rho(y):xDy\},&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]
Then $xDy$ implies $\rho(y)<\rho(x)$. Combined with the no-cycle theorem, this proves that a finite graph is strictly rankable exactly when it is acyclic.

\paragraph{Liar truth table.}
Testing the four Boolean pairs $(Q,L)$ shows that none satisfies both biconditionals. This is a finite illustration of the general propositional proof.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\subsection{Cyclic proof systems}

Cyclic calculi use finite graphs to represent potentially infinite derivations. Their soundness must depend on a global condition over infinite paths. Edgewise descent is too strong because it reduces the system to an acyclic one. A trace condition may instead permit neutral transitions while requiring genuine progress infinitely often along every infinite branch.

\subsection{Recursive programs and productivity}

The unary unravelling resembles a stream that repeatedly emits one value. It is productive because every finite prefix is available after finite work, although the complete stream is never finished. A guarded proof should similarly expose another finite layer of locally correct evidence before reusing a circular hypothesis. Eventual visibility supplies the approximation fact; semantic soundness must connect those layers with truth.

\subsection{Fixed-point semantics}

The observation lattice is suitable for monotone operators and fixed-point constructions. If $F$ maps observations monotonically to observations, least and greatest fixed points may be studied. But $F$ must encode inference correctness, and the choice of fixed point must match the intended derivability. Least fixed points naturally represent inductive, finitely generated behavior; greatest fixed points represent coinductive behavior such as streams. Circular syntax may denote a greatest-fixed-point object without proving a proposition under inductive semantics.

\section{Toward guarded circularity}

\subsection{A later modality}

Introduce $\triangleright A$, read ``$A$ holds one step later.'' Interpret validity at natural indices so that $\triangleright A$ at index $n+1$ may use $A$ only at index $n$. A guarded back-reference consumes an index. Returning to the same syntactic node is then compatible with descent because the semantic state is $(x,n)$ rather than merely $x$.

\subsection{Closure of correctness}

Let $\mathcal O$ be the observation lattice and $\mathcal C\subseteq\mathcal O$ the locally correct observations. One should characterize when $\mathcal C$ is closed under directed unions. For finite-arity local rules, a violation may have a finite witness; directedness would place the entire witness in one stage, contradicting stagewise correctness. Infinitary or branch-global conditions may fail this test.

\subsection{Global trace conditions}

Instead of descent on every edge, associate progress measures with traces and demand infinitely many progress points on every infinite branch. A finite graph may then have cycles while its unravelling forbids infinitely unproductive paths. This is the natural level at which genuine cyclic soundness should be investigated.

\section{Limitations}

The language contains only implication and the derivation system only hypotheses and implication introduction. This is enough to separate identity from circularity, but not a complete logic. The address model permits malformed and inconsistent trees; a complete account must add prefix closure, arity, sequent labels, and local rule correctness.

The no-cycle theorem concerns strict decrease on every dependency edge. It does not refute systems based on guarded delay or global progress. Rather, it explains why those richer conditions are needed. The liar theorem assumes ordinary propositions and exact biconditionals; nonclassical or stratified systems require separate analysis.

\section{Conclusion}

Finite cyclic descriptions, infinite trees, and valid proofs occupy different mathematical layers. A one-node loop can unravel into a well-defined tree with nodes at every finite depth. Finite observations have canonical least upper bounds by union, and every fixed address stabilizes in a sufficiently deep truncation. These facts establish a robust approximation space.

They do not make arbitrary cycles sound. The proof of $P\to P$ is a finite height-one hypothetical derivation, not a proof citing itself. Strict ordinal descent on every dependency edge forbids self-loops and finite cycles, so it cannot validate genuine circularity. Exact reflection combined with the liar equation has no ordinary propositional model.

A viable circular proof theory therefore needs guarded semantic delay, preservation of correctness under limits, or a global progress condition on infinite traces. The observation lattice supplies a space of candidates; soundness determines which candidates deserve to be called proofs.

